1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,844
A PostgreSQL migration can pass review

2
00:00:02,844 --> 00:00:05,269
and still damage data during rollout.

3
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:10,522
The dangerous moment is the compatibility window: a backfill, an old worker, and

4
00:00:10,522 --> 00:00:15,775
a new cutover may each look correct alone, but fail in one ordering.

5
00:00:15,775 --> 00:00:20,220
CutoverProof searches those orderings and executes them against a disposable database.

6
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:23,221
This is the engineer's workspace.

7
00:00:23,221 --> 00:00:25,953
A first-time user can take the short

8
00:00:25,953 --> 00:00:28,685
product tour, then start a safety assessment.

9
00:00:28,685 --> 00:00:32,198
The interface has one clear workflow: bring a migration

10
00:00:32,198 --> 00:00:35,710
pack, watch the test run, and inspect the decision.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:41,179
For this demonstration I upload a fresh JSON file from Downloads.

12
00:00:41,179 --> 00:00:43,991
The pack declares the schema, seed data,

13
00:00:43,991 --> 00:00:47,205
named operations, read-only invariant, and one permitted repair.

14
00:00:47,205 --> 00:00:51,223
The built-in example remains available as a one-click starting point,

15
00:00:51,223 --> 00:00:55,240
but this run uses the uploaded customer file shown here.

16
00:00:56,290 --> 00:00:59,285
The assessment now runs visibly through

17
00:00:59,285 --> 00:01:02,779
validation, planning, execution, verification, and evidence recording.

18
00:01:02,779 --> 00:01:06,773
Gemini proposes bounded schedules using declared operation names.

19
00:01:06,773 --> 00:01:09,268
The gateway rejects invalid plans.

20
00:01:09,268 --> 00:01:14,759
Each accepted candidate starts from a clean PostgreSQL reset, and provider

21
00:01:14,759 --> 00:01:20,250
or infrastructure failures stop the run instead of becoming safety claims.

22
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:23,914
The first candidate finds the failure.

23
00:01:23,914 --> 00:01:28,270
Expand and backfill complete, then a legacy payment write updates

24
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:33,063
the old status text while the new status identifier remains stale.

25
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:36,983
CutoverProof marks the exact conflict on the rollout phases,

26
00:01:36,983 --> 00:01:41,340
blocks cutover, and recommends the compatibility control required before continuing.

27
00:01:42,390 --> 00:01:45,880
The evidence view shows the exact ordering executed

28
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,807
in PostgreSQL and the row returned by the invariant.

29
00:01:49,807 --> 00:01:53,297
Order forty-two says paid in the legacy column,

30
00:01:53,297 --> 00:01:56,787
while status identifier one still resolves to pending.

31
00:01:56,787 --> 00:02:01,150
The model proposes the experiment; deterministic SQL decides what happened.

32
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,627
The detailed timeline preserves the candidate hypothesis, runtime and model

33
00:02:06,627 --> 00:02:11,496
metadata, every executed operation, the failed check, and the returned row.

34
00:02:11,496 --> 00:02:16,808
Raw SQL is available behind each step without overwhelming the main view,

35
00:02:16,808 --> 00:02:22,120
so an engineer can reproduce the decision instead of trusting a screenshot.

36
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:26,452
CutoverProof never applies a consequential change automatically.

37
00:02:26,452 --> 00:02:30,203
The uploaded pack contains one allow-listed compatibility repair.

38
00:02:30,203 --> 00:02:33,954
A named reviewer sees its purpose and boundaries,

39
00:02:33,954 --> 00:02:38,174
then explicitly approves a replay in the disposable sandbox.

40
00:02:38,174 --> 00:02:40,050
Production access remains blocked.

41
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:44,286
After approval, the clean database receives the compatibility

42
00:02:44,286 --> 00:02:47,472
trigger and the identical failing schedule runs again.

43
00:02:47,472 --> 00:02:50,657
This time the invariant returns zero violating rows.

44
00:02:50,657 --> 00:02:54,640
The interface reports repair verified in sandbox; it does not

45
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:59,020
make the broader claim that every possible migration schedule is safe.

46
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:03,770
The same portable record now contains both sides of

47
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:07,881
the safety case: the original counterexample and the human-approved replay.

48
00:03:07,881 --> 00:03:11,170
The operation sequence is identical, the repair is

49
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:14,870
recorded, and the final invariant result is zero rows.

50
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,267
Here is the complete trust boundary.

51
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:20,613
The React portal and named reviewer

52
00:03:20,613 --> 00:03:23,351
call the FastAPI workflow on Cloud Run.

53
00:03:23,351 --> 00:03:27,653
The pack validator and tool gateway constrain what Gemini may propose.

54
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:32,347
PostgreSQL executes each schedule, the SQL verifier decides pass or block, and

55
00:03:32,347 --> 00:03:37,040
the evidence recorder links every result to the human approval and replay.

56
00:03:38,090 --> 00:03:44,340
Under the same model, facts, seed, and four-candidate budget, the specialised agent found

57
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:51,070
all three unsafe benchmark scenarios with no false alarms and mean search effort one.

58
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:53,954
The one-shot baseline found two of

59
00:03:53,954 --> 00:03:57,320
three, while the heuristic required three candidates.

60
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:00,685
The measured advantage is earlier semantic schedule

61
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:04,050
selection, not a claim of exhaustive safety.

62
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:07,531
Evaluation strengthened four trust boundaries.

63
00:04:07,531 --> 00:04:10,935
Provider and parser errors now fail closed.

64
00:04:10,935 --> 00:04:14,338
Schedule selection and repair reasoning are separated.

65
00:04:14,338 --> 00:04:16,283
Invalid experiments remain visible.

66
00:04:16,283 --> 00:04:19,687
And the customer import path produces the

67
00:04:19,687 --> 00:04:23,090
same PostgreSQL evidence and bounded approval record.

68
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:26,980
Every benchmark number stays tied to executed evidence.

69
00:04:28,030 --> 00:04:34,410
CutoverProof gives backend engineers a concrete answer before production: not just whether migration steps look valid, but which

70
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:40,790
real ordering breaks the rollout, what row proves it, and whether an approved repair survives the same test.
